-- title:
--   Truncation nodes for finite witnesses
-- statement:
--   These notions belong to a proof that every reflexive real Banach space has the fixed point property for nonexpansive maps: every $F:C\to C$ with $\|Fa-Fb\|\le\|a-b\|$ on a nonempty closed bounded convex set $C$ has a fixed point. The proof assumes a fixed-point-free map, reduces to a minimal invariant set $K$ of diameter $1$, builds an adaptive weighted tree of nonexpansive maps generated by resolvents, and contradicts the minimality through detecting functionals. An ordered tree has finite levels $0,1,2,\dots$ with a single root, and monotone surjective parent maps from level $k+1$ to level $k$; edge weights are positive and sum to $1$ over the children of each parent. Each node carries a nonexpansive selfmap of $K$, and the tree is evaluated bottom-up: a node's value is its map applied to the weighted average of its children's values.
--
--   1. **Truncation nodes.** For a depth $N$, the nonroot nodes at levels $1,\dots,N$, as pairs $(k,v)$ with $k<N$ and $v$ at level $k+1$. Each satisfies $k+1\le N$.
--   2. **Finite witness principle.** Suppose block detection holds: each truncation node $v$ has $\varphi_v(\text{before}-\text{after})\ge\pi(v)-5e_v$ for the switching experiment at depth $N$, where $\sum_ve_v\le\frac1{128}$. Then some leaf's normalized switch vector $u$ lies in the unit ball of the difference span and satisfies
--   $$
--   \varphi_v(u)\ge\tfrac12\quad\text{for every truncation node }v\text{ on its path}.
--   $$
